PATCH 532: the three-call cockpit-HUD blocks in FUN_1000_8cbf and FUN_1000_93f3 (board:0014).

Both functions end their frame with the same three vector calls, and the asm sets AX and BX explicitly
before each one:

    1000:8cbf   18d40: mov bx,0x91c8 ; mov ax,0    ; lcall *0x6d8
                18d4a: mov bx,0x91b8 ; mov ax,0x18 ; lcall *0x6c8
                18d54: mov bx,0x91c0 ; mov ax,0x18 ; lcall *0x6d0
    1000:93f3   19470: mov bx,0x92e6 ; mov ax,0    ; lcall *0x6d8
                1947a: mov bx,0x92d6 ; mov ax,0x18 ; lcall *0x6c8
                19484: mov bx,0x92de ; mov ax,0x18 ; lcall *0x6d0

The descriptors match the DGROUP globals the same C block writes just above (DAT_2000_51c8/51cc =
0x91c8/0x91cc, DAT_2000_52e6/52ea = 0x92e6/0x92ea), which confirms the reading.

Three defects, six sites:

  * `[0x6d8]` -> FUN_0000_2ae9.  The call passed `(0, unaff_CS, uVar3)`: `unaff_CS` sat in param_2 --
    which patch 312 states outright is BX, the position descriptor -- and it is an UNINITIALISED
    pseudo-var, i.e. the patch-525 class: undefined behaviour, and a different value under gcc -m32
    than under emcc.  Now `(0, <desc>, 0)`; param_3 is unused by 2ae9's body.
  * `[0x6c8]` -> FUN_0000_294d (five params, ax=param_1, bx=param_4).  Passed `(0)`; AX is 0x18 here,
    not 0, and the descriptor was missing entirely.  Now `(0x18,0,0,<desc>,0)`.
  * `[0x6d0]` -> FUN_0000_2a39 (four params).  Passed `(0)`.  Now `(0x18,0,<desc>,0)`.

IMPORTANT, and the reason each target must be read individually: the `__allregs` parameter order is
PER-FUNCTION.  BX is param_4 in 294d, param_2 in 2ae9, and param_3 in 2a39 -- the last established by
2a39's own reconstruction (patch 505), whose body calls `FUN_0000_260c(param_1,param_3,..)` where 2ae9's
calls `FUN_0000_260c(param_1,param_2,..)`.  Generalising a single (ax,bx) convention across these
vectors would silently mis-place the descriptor.

NOT touched, and left on board:0014: the third member of this family, FUN_1000_6f1f (asm 0x16f1f, the
mode-0 view whose INIT sets word[0x1552]=0x8d60).  Its block has the same three calls, but AX at its
[0x6d8] site is branch-dependent -- `16fc7: mov ax,8 ; testb [di+0x1a],0x10 ; je ; mov ax,0x10` on one
path and `16fe1: mov ax,0` on the path through the [0x6c8]/[0x6d0] pair -- and the decompile dropped
that selection as well as the arity.  Fixing it is a reconstruction, not an arity correction, and it
needs the `di = word[0x6d34]` player-vehicle base threading with it.

